   "subject": "Re: manymome question #2",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>In this model, angfrus and calm are parallel mediators.</div><div><br></div><div><font face=\"Courier New\">one_ind &lt;- &#39;disob_f ~ home_f + angfrus + calm # c and b paths</font></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><font face=\"Courier New\"><br>            angfrus ~ home_f<br>            calm ~ home_f # a path<br>            calm~~angfrus&#39;<br><br></font></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><font face=\"Courier New\"></font></div><div>They are two different paths:<br></div><div><font face=\"Courier New\"><br></font></div><div>home_f -&gt; angfrus -&gt; disob_f<br></div><div>home_f -&gt; calm -&gt; disob_f<br></div><div><font face=\"Courier New\"><br></font></div><div>This will work:</div><div><br></div><div><font face=\"Courier New\">out_fit1_angfrus &lt;- indirect_effect(x = &quot;home_f&quot;,<br>                                    m = c(&quot;angfrus&quot;),<br>                                    y = &quot;disob_f&quot;,<br>                                    fit = fit1,<br>                                    boot_ci = TRUE,<br>                                    R = 10,</font></div><div><font face=\"Courier New\">                                    seed = 2345)<br>out_fit1_clam &lt;- indirect_effect(x = &quot;home_f&quot;,<br>                                 m = c(&quot;calm&quot;),<br>                                 y = &quot;disob_f&quot;,<br>                                 fit = fit1,<br>                                 boot_ci = TRUE,<br>                                 R = 10,</font></div><div><font face=\"Courier New\">                                 seed = 2345)</font><br></div><div><br></div><div>Make sure that you set the seed, to ensure that the same B bootstrap samples are used across analyses, or use do_boot() to generate bootstrap estimates that can be reused across analyses. (But see the next paragraph on bootstrapping with clustering ...)</div><div><br></div><div>By the way, I noticed that you used cluster. I am not sure how lavaan handles bootstrapping when &#39;cluster&#39; is used. Bootstrapping conducted in manymome tries to mimic what lavaan does, to ensure the results are consistent with those of lavaan. If done internally, manymome uses casewise resampling, which ignores clustering. I am not sure how lavaan does bootstrapping when the argument &#39;cluster&#39; is used. (I would like to know more about this but will ask in another post.)</div><div><br></div><div>If you would like to form CIs for the indirect effects while using MLR with clustering in sem(), you can use the Monte Carlo confidence intervals. It uses the variance-covariance matrix generated by lavaan to form the Monte Carlo CI intervals, and so you can use whatever estimation method you like when calling sem(). E.g.,</div><div><font face=\"Courier New\"><br></font></div><div><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">out_fit1_angfrus &lt;- indirect_effect(x = &quot;home_f&quot;,</span><br style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">                                    m = c(&quot;angfrus&quot;),</span><br style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">                                    y = &quot;disob_f&quot;,</span><br style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">                                    fit = fit1,</span><br style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">                                    mc_ci = TRUE,</span><br style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">                                    R = 10,</span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">                                    seed = 4567)</span><br></div><br>Again, the two mediators are in parallel and you need two calls to indirect_effect(). You should set the seed to the same number, or pregenerage the MC simulated estimates using do_mc() and reuse the output (<a href=\"https://sfcheung.github.io/manymome/articles/do_mc.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://sfcheung.github.io/manymome/articles/do_mc.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414335048000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0yeZ85lNmiOLIgaiL1hamH\">https://sfcheung.github.io/manymome/articles/do_mc.html</a>).<div><br><div>Hope this helps.<div><br></div><div>-- Shu Fai</div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
